# Backend API origin. Only VITE_-prefixed variables reach browser code — never put a secret
# here, since anything in this file ends up readable in the shipped JS bundle.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRODUCTION (Vercel) — set in the Vercel dashboard, NOT here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=
#   Under the Vercel Services setup in /vercel.json (frontend+backend sharing one domain), the
#   correct production value is a relative, same-origin base — and LEAVING THIS VARIABLE UNSET
#   IS NOW CORRECT AND SUFFICIENT. Nothing needs to be added in the Vercel dashboard.
#
#   This was NOT true originally, and the history is worth knowing if this ever regresses:
#   api/client.js used to read `import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE_URL` with no fallback, and Vite
#   substitutes that expression at BUILD time — an unset var compiled to `void 0` (undefined), so
#   every request fetched the literal string "undefined/api/auth/login" and 404'd, discovered
#   exactly this way against the first real Vercel deploy (see LEARNING_LOG.md). client.js was
#   then fixed to `import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE_URL ?? ''` — verified against a real production
#   build that this compiles to `""` even with the variable completely absent, so every request
#   now correctly fetches a same-origin relative path (e.g. "/api/auth/login").
#
#   Vercel's dashboard also would not accept an empty-but-present value for a Secret-type env
#   var ("Enter a value" validation error) — another reason the `?? ''` fallback in code is the
#   right fix, not a workaround: it removes the need to fight the dashboard into accepting blank
#   at all.
#
#   A split deployment (frontend and backend on separate domains) is the other valid setup; then
#   THIS DOES need to be set, to the backend's full origin, e.g. https://<backend>.vercel.app,
#   with no trailing slash — client.js concatenates it directly onto paths that already start
#   with "/".
